ITEMS = [
    Q('what-testng', 'Basics', 'e',
      'What is TestNG and why would you choose it over JUnit 4?',
      'NG stands for Next Generation. Think of what it added for large test suites.',
      'TestNG is a Java testing framework inspired by JUnit and NUnit. Compared with JUnit 4 it adds suite level setup, groups, test dependencies, data providers, parameters from XML, built in parallel execution, listeners and HTML reports, which make it popular for Selenium and API automation suites.',
      '<p>JUnit 5 has closed much of the gap with tags, parameterised tests and extensions. TestNG is still common in automation because of testng.xml suites, <code>dependsOnMethods</code> and simple parallel settings.</p>',
      follow=['Have you used JUnit 5? What would you miss from TestNG?']),
    Q('annotation-order', 'Annotations', 'e',
      'What is the execution order of TestNG annotations?',
      'Go from the biggest container to the smallest, then back out.',
      '<code>@BeforeSuite</code>, <code>@BeforeTest</code>, <code>@BeforeClass</code>, <code>@BeforeMethod</code>, <code>@Test</code>, <code>@AfterMethod</code>, <code>@AfterClass</code>, <code>@AfterTest</code>, <code>@AfterSuite</code>. <code>@BeforeGroups</code> and <code>@AfterGroups</code> run before the first and after the last test method of a group.',
      '<p>Suite means the whole <code>&lt;suite&gt;</code> in testng.xml, Test means one <code>&lt;test&gt;</code> tag inside it, which may contain many classes, Class means each test class, and Method wraps each <code>@Test</code> method, including each row from a data provider.</p><p>A typical framework opens the report in <code>@BeforeSuite</code>, reads config in <code>@BeforeTest</code>, starts the browser in <code>@BeforeMethod</code> and quits it in <code>@AfterMethod</code>.</p>',
      points=['Suite, Test, Class, Method, then the test', 'After annotations run in the reverse order', '@BeforeMethod runs before every @Test']),
    Q('order-output', 'Annotations', 'm', 'What does this class print when it is run on its own?',
      'BeforeMethod and AfterMethod wrap each test. Tests run in alphabetical order by default.',
      'It prints <code>BC</code>, <code>BM</code>, <code>a</code>, <code>AM</code>, <code>BM</code>, <code>b</code>, <code>AM</code>, <code>AC</code>. Without priorities TestNG runs test methods in alphabetical order of their names, and the method level setup and teardown run around each test.',
      '<p>Do not rely on alphabetical order in real suites. Use <code>priority</code> or <code>dependsOnMethods</code> when order matters, and better still keep tests independent.</p>',
      kind='Output',
      code='public class OrderTest {\n    @BeforeClass  public void bc() { System.out.println("BC"); }\n    @BeforeMethod public void bm() { System.out.println("BM"); }\n    @Test public void b() { System.out.println("b"); }\n    @Test public void a() { System.out.println("a"); }\n    @AfterMethod  public void am() { System.out.println("AM"); }\n    @AfterClass   public void ac() { System.out.println("AC"); }\n}',
      acode='BC\nBM\na\nAM\nBM\nb\nAM\nAC'),
    Q('test-attributes', 'Annotations', 'e',
      'Name the most useful attributes of the <code>@Test</code> annotation.',
      'Think of order, skipping, grouping, data, timing and expected failures.',
      '<code>priority</code>, <code>enabled</code>, <code>groups</code>, <code>dependsOnMethods</code>, <code>dependsOnGroups</code>, <code>dataProvider</code>, <code>timeOut</code>, <code>invocationCount</code>, <code>threadPoolSize</code>, <code>expectedExceptions</code>, <code>alwaysRun</code>, <code>description</code> and <code>retryAnalyzer</code>.',
      '<p>Examples: <code>@Test(enabled = false)</code> skips a test that is known to be broken, <code>@Test(timeOut = 5000)</code> fails a test that runs longer than 5 seconds, and <code>@Test(expectedExceptions = IllegalArgumentException.class)</code> passes only if that exception is thrown.</p>'),
    Q('priority', 'Annotations', 'm',
      'How does <code>priority</code> work? What happens with negative values, equal values and tests without a priority?',
      'Lower numbers run first, and the default value is 0.',
      'Tests run from the lowest priority number to the highest. Negative values are allowed and run before 0. A test without a priority has the default of 0. Tests with the same priority run in alphabetical order of their method names.',
      '<p>Priority only orders tests, it does not create a dependency. If a high priority login test fails, a later test still runs. Use <code>dependsOnMethods</code> if a test should be skipped when another fails.</p>'),
    Q('depends-on', 'Annotations', 'm',
      'What is the difference between <code>dependsOnMethods</code> and <code>priority</code>? What does <code>alwaysRun</code> do?',
      'One of them skips tests when an earlier one fails.',
      '<code>dependsOnMethods</code> makes a test wait for other methods and skips it if any of them failed. <code>priority</code> only sets the order. <code>alwaysRun = true</code> on a dependent test makes it run even if the methods it depends on failed. On a configuration method such as <code>@AfterMethod</code> it makes the method run regardless of groups and failures.',
      '<p>A soft dependency, written with <code>alwaysRun = true</code>, keeps the order but not the skipping. Use <code>alwaysRun = true</code> on teardown so the browser is closed even when a test or setup failed or when you run only some groups.</p>'),
    Q('enabled-skip', 'Annotations', 'e',
      'How can you skip a test in TestNG?',
      'There is an attribute, an exception and an XML option.',
      'Set <code>@Test(enabled = false)</code>, throw <code>new SkipException("reason")</code> inside the test (useful for conditional skips such as "feature not on this environment"), or exclude the method or its group in testng.xml.',
      '<p>A test is also reported as skipped when a method it depends on fails, or when a <code>@BeforeMethod</code> fails, since the setup it needed did not complete.</p>'),

    Q('testng-xml', 'testng.xml', 'e',
      'What is testng.xml and what can you configure in it?',
      'Suites, tests, classes, groups, parameters, listeners, parallel.',
      'testng.xml defines what to run and how. In it you set the suite, one or more tests, the classes, packages or methods inside each test, groups to include or exclude, parameters, listeners, and parallel settings such as <code>parallel</code> and <code>thread-count</code>.',
      '<p>Keeping several XML files, such as smoke.xml and regression.xml, lets CI choose a suite without code changes.</p>',
      acode='<suite name="Regression" parallel="classes" thread-count="4">\n  <listeners>\n    <listener class-name="com.shop.listeners.TestListener"/>\n  </listeners>\n  <test name="Chrome">\n    <parameter name="browser" value="chrome"/>\n    <groups><run><include name="smoke"/></run></groups>\n    <classes>\n      <class name="com.shop.tests.LoginTest"/>\n      <class name="com.shop.tests.CartTest"/>\n    </classes>\n  </test>\n</suite>'),
    Q('parameters', 'testng.xml', 'm',
      'How do you pass values such as browser and URL from testng.xml to a test?',
      'An annotation reads a <code>&lt;parameter&gt;</code> tag.',
      'Declare <code>&lt;parameter name="browser" value="chrome"/&gt;</code> in the suite or test tag, and read it with <code>@Parameters("browser")</code> on a test or configuration method that takes a matching argument. <code>@Optional("chrome")</code> gives a default when the XML does not set it.',
      '<p>A parameter at <code>&lt;test&gt;</code> level overrides one with the same name at <code>&lt;suite&gt;</code> level. For values that change per CI run, many teams read <code>System.getProperty("browser")</code> instead, passed with <code>-Dbrowser=firefox</code> on the Maven command line.</p>',
      acode='@Parameters({"browser", "baseUrl"})\n@BeforeMethod\npublic void setUp(@Optional("chrome") String browser, String baseUrl) {\n    DriverManager.start(browser);\n    DriverManager.get().get(baseUrl);\n}'),
    Q('groups', 'testng.xml', 'm',
      'How do groups work, and how do you run only smoke tests?',
      'Tag the methods, then include or exclude in XML.',
      'Tag tests with <code>@Test(groups = {"smoke", "regression"})</code>. In testng.xml use <code>&lt;groups&gt;&lt;run&gt;&lt;include name="smoke"/&gt;&lt;/run&gt;&lt;/groups&gt;</code>. Exclude wins over include, so a test in both an included and an excluded group does not run.',
      '<p>Configuration methods such as <code>@BeforeMethod</code> do not run for a group selection unless they belong to that group or have <code>alwaysRun = true</code>. This is the most common reason the browser does not open when running only smoke tests. Groups can also be chosen from Maven with <code>-Dgroups=smoke</code>.</p>'),
    Q('suite-vs-test', 'testng.xml', 'm',
      'What is the difference between <code>@BeforeTest</code> and <code>@BeforeMethod</code>?',
      'One of them matches a tag in testng.xml.',
      '<code>@BeforeTest</code> runs once before all classes inside one <code>&lt;test&gt;</code> tag of testng.xml. <code>@BeforeMethod</code> runs before every single <code>@Test</code> method.',
      '<p>People often confuse "Test" in <code>@BeforeTest</code> with a test method. If a <code>&lt;test&gt;</code> tag contains three classes with ten methods, <code>@BeforeTest</code> runs once and <code>@BeforeMethod</code> runs ten times.</p>'),

    Q('dataprovider', 'Data driven testing', 'e',
      'What is a <code>@DataProvider</code> and how do you use it?',
      'A method that returns rows of arguments.',
      'A <code>@DataProvider</code> method returns <code>Object[][]</code> or <code>Iterator&lt;Object[]&gt;</code>, where each row is one set of arguments. A test with <code>@Test(dataProvider = "name")</code> runs once per row, and each run is reported separately.',
      '<p>The provider can live in another class if you add <code>dataProviderClass = LoginData.class</code> and make the provider method <code>static</code>. Returning an <code>Iterator</code> loads rows lazily, which helps with large files.</p>',
      acode='@DataProvider(name = "logins")\npublic Object[][] logins() {\n    return new Object[][] {\n        {"standard_user", "secret", true},\n        {"locked_user",   "secret", false},\n    };\n}\n\n@Test(dataProvider = "logins")\npublic void login(String user, String pass, boolean ok) {\n    assertEquals(new LoginPage().login(user, pass).isLoggedIn(), ok);\n}'),
    Q('dataprovider-vs-parameters', 'Data driven testing', 'm',
      'What is the difference between <code>@Parameters</code> and <code>@DataProvider</code>?',
      'How many times does the test run with each one?',
      '<code>@Parameters</code> passes single values from testng.xml, so the test runs once with them. It suits environment settings such as the browser. <code>@DataProvider</code> supplies many rows from Java code, so the test runs once per row. It suits test data such as many logins.',
      '<p>A data provider can be fed from Excel with Apache POI, CSV, JSON or a database, which is how most data driven frameworks are built.</p>'),
    Q('dp-parallel', 'Data driven testing', 'h',
      'How do you run data provider rows in parallel?',
      'There is an attribute on the provider and a setting in testng.xml.',
      'Set <code>@DataProvider(parallel = true)</code>. TestNG then runs the rows on a separate pool whose size is <code>data-provider-thread-count</code> in the suite tag, which is 10 by default.',
      '<p>Each row then runs on its own thread, so the driver must be in a <code>ThreadLocal</code> and test data must not be shared and changed between rows.</p>'),
    Q('excel-data', 'Data driven testing', 'h',
      'Your manager wants test data in Excel. How would you connect it to TestNG?',
      'Apache POI reads the sheet, and a data provider hands the rows to the test.',
      'Write a utility that opens the workbook with Apache POI, reads the sheet row by row with <code>DataFormatter</code> so numbers and dates come back as text, and returns an <code>Object[][]</code>. Call it from a <code>@DataProvider</code>, and point the test at that provider.',
      '<p>Good practices: keep the file path in config, close the workbook with try with resources, skip the header row, and give each row a test case id so the report shows which row failed. For large data, return an <code>Iterator&lt;Object[]&gt;</code>.</p>',
      kind='Scenario'),

    Q('hard-soft', 'Assertions', 'e',
      'What is the difference between hard and soft assertions?',
      'One stops the test at the first failure.',
      'A hard assertion from <code>Assert</code> throws at the first failure and stops the test. A <code>SoftAssert</code> records failures and lets the test continue, and only <code>softAssert.assertAll()</code> at the end reports them and fails the test.',
      '<p>Forgetting <code>assertAll()</code> means the test passes even with failed checks, which is a common bug. Create a new <code>SoftAssert</code> inside each test method rather than sharing one as a field, or failures from one test leak into the next.</p>',
      acode='SoftAssert soft = new SoftAssert();\nsoft.assertEquals(page.title(), "Cart");\nsoft.assertTrue(page.hasCheckoutButton(), "checkout button");\nsoft.assertAll();'),
    Q('assert-methods', 'Assertions', 'e',
      'Which TestNG assertion methods do you use most?',
      'Equality, truth, null checks and failing on purpose.',
      '<code>assertEquals(actual, expected, message)</code>, <code>assertNotEquals</code>, <code>assertTrue</code>, <code>assertFalse</code>, <code>assertNull</code>, <code>assertNotNull</code>, <code>assertSame</code> and <code>fail(message)</code>. Since TestNG 7 there is also <code>assertThrows</code> and <code>expectThrows</code>.',
      '<p>In TestNG the order is actual first, then expected, which is the reverse of JUnit. Getting it backwards makes failure messages confusing. Always add a message so the report explains what was being checked.</p>'),

    Q('listeners', 'Listeners and reporting', 'm',
      'What are TestNG listeners? Which ones have you used?',
      'Interfaces whose methods TestNG calls at events such as test failure.',
      'Listeners are interfaces that TestNG calls back at events. <code>ITestListener</code> has <code>onTestStart</code>, <code>onTestSuccess</code>, <code>onTestFailure</code> and <code>onTestSkipped</code>, and is used for screenshots and reports. <code>ISuiteListener</code> runs at suite start and end, <code>IRetryAnalyzer</code> retries failures, <code>IAnnotationTransformer</code> changes annotations at run time, <code>IReporter</code> builds custom reports, and <code>IInvokedMethodListener</code> runs around every method.',
      '<p>Register listeners in testng.xml with <code>&lt;listeners&gt;</code>, with <code>@Listeners</code> on a class, or through the service loader file. The XML way applies to the whole suite and keeps tests clean.</p>'),
    Q('screenshot-fail', 'Listeners and reporting', 'm',
      'How do you take a screenshot automatically when a test fails?',
      'Use a listener method that is called on failure.',
      'Implement <code>ITestListener.onTestFailure(ITestResult result)</code>, get the driver for the current thread, cast it to <code>TakesScreenshot</code>, save the file named after <code>result.getName()</code> and a time stamp, and attach it to the report.',
      '<p>With parallel runs, get the driver from your <code>ThreadLocal</code> manager, not from a static field. With Extent or Allure, attach the screenshot as Base64 so the report is a single file you can share.</p>',
      kind='Scenario',
      acode='public class TestListener implements ITestListener {\n    @Override\n    public void onTestFailure(ITestResult result) {\n        File src = ((TakesScreenshot) DriverManager.get()).getScreenshotAs(OutputType.FILE);\n        Path to = Path.of("screenshots", result.getName() + "_" + System.currentTimeMillis() + ".png");\n        try { Files.createDirectories(to.getParent()); Files.copy(src.toPath(), to); }\n        catch (IOException e) { throw new UncheckedIOException(e); }\n    }\n}'),
    Q('retry', 'Listeners and reporting', 'h',
      'How do you rerun failed tests in TestNG?',
      'There is a per test way and a whole suite way.',
      'Implement <code>IRetryAnalyzer</code>, whose <code>retry(ITestResult)</code> returns true while a retry count is below the limit, and attach it with <code>@Test(retryAnalyzer = Retry.class)</code>. To apply it to every test, set it in an <code>IAnnotationTransformer</code>. After a run TestNG also writes <code>testng-failed.xml</code> in the output folder, which you can run to repeat only the failures.',
      '<p>Retries hide flakiness, so log every retry and fix the cause, such as missing waits, shared data or test order dependencies. Keep the limit small, one or two tries.</p>',
      acode='public class Retry implements IRetryAnalyzer {\n    private int count = 0;\n    private static final int MAX = 2;\n    @Override\n    public boolean retry(ITestResult result) {\n        return count++ < MAX;\n    }\n}'),
    Q('reports', 'Listeners and reporting', 'e',
      'What reports does TestNG create, and which reporting tools have you used with it?',
      'Look in the test-output folder, then think of nicer third party reports.',
      'TestNG writes <code>index.html</code>, <code>emailable-report.html</code> and <code>testng-results.xml</code> in <code>test-output</code> (or under <code>target/surefire-reports</code> with Maven). Teams usually add Extent Reports or Allure for richer reports with steps, screenshots and history.',
      '<p>CI servers such as Jenkins read <code>testng-results.xml</code> or the JUnit style XML to show trends and failed tests.</p>'),

    Q('parallel', 'Parallel execution', 'm',
      'How do you run tests in parallel, and what values can <code>parallel</code> take?',
      'Set it on the suite tag together with a thread count.',
      'Set <code>parallel</code> and <code>thread-count</code> on the <code>&lt;suite&gt;</code> tag. <code>parallel</code> can be <code>methods</code>, <code>classes</code>, <code>tests</code> or <code>instances</code>. <code>thread-count</code> is the size of the pool.',
      '<table><tr><th>Value</th><th>What runs at the same time</th></tr><tr><td>tests</td><td>Each <code>&lt;test&gt;</code> tag on its own thread, handy for one browser per test tag</td></tr><tr><td>classes</td><td>Each class on its own thread, methods inside a class run one after another</td></tr><tr><td>methods</td><td>Every test method on any free thread, the most parallel and the most likely to expose shared state</td></tr><tr><td>instances</td><td>Methods of the same instance on the same thread</td></tr></table>'),
    Q('parallel-flaky', 'Parallel execution', 'h',
      'After switching on <code>parallel="methods"</code>, tests start failing with stale sessions and wrong pages. What would you check?',
      'Anything static or shared between tests is now shared between threads.',
      'Check for shared state. The usual cause is a static <code>WebDriver</code>, so move it to a <code>ThreadLocal</code>. Also check page objects stored as fields of the test class, shared test data such as one user account used by every test, a <code>SoftAssert</code> shared as a field, and report or logger objects that are not thread safe.',
      '<p>Each test should create its own data, such as a unique email, and clean up after itself. If tests in one class depend on each other, run that class with <code>parallel="classes"</code> instead, or remove the dependency.</p>',
      kind='Scenario'),
    Q('invocation', 'Parallel execution', 'm',
      'What do <code>invocationCount</code> and <code>threadPoolSize</code> do?',
      'Run the same test many times.',
      '<code>invocationCount = 5</code> runs the same test method five times. <code>threadPoolSize = 3</code> runs those invocations on three threads at the same time. This is useful for a quick load check or for finding a flaky test.',
      '<p>It does not replace real performance tools such as JMeter or k6, but it is a cheap way to reproduce race conditions.</p>'),

    Q('factory', 'Advanced', 'h',
      'What is <code>@Factory</code> and how is it different from <code>@DataProvider</code>?',
      'One creates test class instances, the other passes arguments to a method.',
      '<code>@Factory</code> returns an array of test class instances, so every <code>@Test</code> method of the class runs once for each instance with that instance\'s fields. <code>@DataProvider</code> runs one test method many times with different arguments.',
      '<p>Use a factory when a whole class should run with different setups, for example the same checkout tests for each country or each user role. A factory can itself use a data provider to build its instances.</p>'),
    Q('expected-exceptions', 'Advanced', 'm',
      'How do you test that a method throws an exception?',
      'There is an attribute and there is an assertion.',
      'Use <code>@Test(expectedExceptions = InvalidCouponException.class, expectedExceptionsMessageRegExp = ".*expired.*")</code>, or call <code>Assert.assertThrows(InvalidCouponException.class, () -&gt; cart.apply("OLD10"))</code> inside the test.',
      '<p><code>assertThrows</code> is more precise, because the attribute passes if any line in the test throws that exception, even a setup line you did not mean to test.</p>'),
    Q('maven', 'Maven and CI', 'm',
      'How do you run TestNG suites from Maven and in CI?',
      'The Surefire plugin can point at testng.xml files.',
      'Add the <code>maven-surefire-plugin</code> to pom.xml with <code>&lt;suiteXmlFiles&gt;</code> pointing at the XML file, then run <code>mvn test</code>. Make the file a property so CI can pick the suite, for example <code>mvn test -DsuiteXmlFile=smoke.xml -Dbrowser=chrome</code>.',
      '<p>In Jenkins or GitHub Actions, run the Maven command, publish the TestNG or Surefire XML results, and archive the HTML report and screenshots. Run headless browsers in CI and set a sensible <code>thread-count</code> for the agent size.</p>',
      acode='<plugin>\n  <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>\n  <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>\n  <configuration>\n    <suiteXmlFiles>\n      <suiteXmlFile>${suiteXmlFile}</suiteXmlFile>\n    </suiteXmlFiles>\n  </configuration>\n</plugin>'),
    Q('framework', 'Maven and CI', 'x',
      'Explain how TestNG fits into your Selenium framework, from the command line to the report.',
      'Walk the path: Maven, testng.xml, listeners, base test, page objects, assertions, report.',
      'Maven Surefire starts the suite named on the command line. testng.xml chooses the groups, parameters, listeners and parallel mode. A <code>BaseTest</code> uses <code>@BeforeMethod</code> to create a thread safe driver from the browser parameter and <code>@AfterMethod(alwaysRun = true)</code> to quit it. Tests call page objects and use data providers for data. Listeners log each step, take screenshots on failure and retry known flaky tests. The report and screenshots are archived in CI.',
      '<p>Mention the reasons: XML suites let CI choose smoke or regression without code changes, ThreadLocal keeps parallel runs safe, alwaysRun teardown prevents browsers being left open, and listeners keep reporting code out of the tests.</p>',
      kind='Design', follow=['How long does your regression suite take and how did you reduce it?', 'How do you handle test data cleanup?']),
    Q('beforemethod-fail', 'Advanced', 'h',
      'What happens to the tests when a <code>@BeforeMethod</code> or <code>@BeforeClass</code> fails?',
      'The tests do not fail, but they do not pass either.',
      'The tests that needed that setup are skipped, not failed. If <code>@BeforeClass</code> fails, every test in the class is skipped. If <code>@BeforeSuite</code> fails, everything in the suite is skipped. By default, after one configuration failure TestNG also skips later configuration methods of the same kind.',
      '<p>Set <code>configfailurepolicy="continue"</code> on the suite tag to keep trying configuration methods for later tests. Watch the skipped count in reports, since a large number of skips usually means a setup problem, not passing tests.</p>'),
]
